<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Sovereign for whom? | Canada2080"
    description="Dr Kimberly Yazzie on sovereign AI as a question of relationships, consent, and partnership, from her cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Sovereign AI · 1:20 p.m."
    headline="Sovereign for whom?"
    name="Dr Kimberly Yazzie"
    bio="Dr Kimberly Yazzie is an Assistant Professor in the Department of Forests and Conservation Sciences at the University of British Columbia. Her research spans freshwater ecology, climate solutions, and Indigenous land and water governance."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/kimberly-yazzie-ph-d-09758a4a/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5373.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Dr Kimberly Yazzie on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely."
    photos={[
        {
            src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5373.jpg",
            alt: "Dr Kimberly Yazzie on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely.",
            caption: "On the screen.",
        },
    ]}
>
    <p class="lede">
        Sovereign AI, she said, is usually a question of who owns the compute,
        the data, and the models. She asked a different one: for whom?
    </p>
    <p>
        She spoke in her own capacity, not on behalf of her institution, and
        thanked the organizers for bringing education, industry, and governance
        into one room.
    </p>
    <p>
        For Indigenous communities, she said, AI is a question of
        relationships. Who decides, who benefits, and who carries the
        responsibility long after a project ends.
    </p>
    <p>
        Satellites and sensors watching over a community's land can be a tool
        for care. Built without the people of that land, the same system can
        become a sophisticated way of watching them. She said it is not her
        place to speak for any community. What she can say is that Indigenous
        data sovereignty and early engagement deserve real support.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every community can take up a new technology, or not, on its own terms.
        When a community chooses to step in, that begins with education and
        partnership from the first conversation, not after the design is
        finished.
    </p>
    <p>Two commitments make that choice meaningful.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Consent that stays open. Free, prior, and informed consent is an
            ongoing relationship, not a checkbox at the start. A community can
            revisit its choice as the work grows. Data shared along the way
            stays under the authority of the community it describes.
        </li>
        <li>
            Accountability to relationships, not just outputs. Indigenous
            scholars have long taught that resilience lives in relationships of
            care and responsibility with land, water, and non-human relatives.
            AI cannot move in a single direction set from the outside. It has
            to move with Indigenous governance, and that governance needs real
            space.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        She cited the UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial
        Intelligence, adopted by member states as the first global standard in
        AI ethics. It recognizes that Indigenous peoples must take part in
        shaping AI. The task, she said, is to make room for that in practice.
    </p>
    <p>
        Her proposal: begin with the partnership, not a product. Build the
        education, the trust, and the governance together, then see where the
        relationship goes. Technologies will change. What endures are the
        relationships each generation carries forward.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Hi everyone. Thank you Francis. Thank you for that
                introduction. Thank you to the organizers for bringing
                education, industry, and governance into one room.
            </p>
            <p>
                I speak today in my own capacity, not on behalf of my
                institution.
            </p>
            <p>
                In some cases, when we talk about sovereign AI, we usually mean
                who owns the compute, the data, and the models. I want to ask a
                different question: for whom?
            </p>
            <p>
                For Indigenous communities, AI is not only a technical
                question. It is a question of relationships. Who decides, who
                benefits, and who carries the responsibility long after a
                project or collaboration ends?
            </p>
            <p>
                Take a system that uses satellites and sensors to watch over a
                community's land. It can be a powerful tool for care, or, if it
                is built without the people of that land, it can become a very
                sophisticated way of watching them. It is not my place to speak
                for any community, but what I can say is that Indigenous data
                sovereignty and early engagement deserve real support.
            </p>
            <p>
                Every community has a choice to take up new technology or not
                on their own terms. When a community chooses to step in, it
                begins with education and partnership from the very first
                conversation, not after the design or collaboration is
                finished.
            </p>
            <p>Two commitments make that choice meaningful.</p>
            <p>
                First, consent that stays open. Free, prior, and informed
                consent is not a checkbox at the start of a project. It is an
                ongoing relationship, and a community can revisit its choice as
                the work grows. Data shared along the way should remain under
                the authority of the community it describes.
            </p>
            <p>
                Second, accountability to relationships, not just outputs.
                Indigenous scholars have long taught that resilience lives in
                relationships of care and responsibility with land, water, and
                non-human relatives. This means AI development cannot move in a
                single direction set from the outside. It needs to move in
                tandem with Indigenous governance, with that governance
                acknowledged and given real space.
            </p>
            <p>
                The UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial
                Intelligence, adopted by UNESCO's member states as the first
                global standard in AI ethics, recognizes that Indigenous
                peoples must take part in shaping AI. Our task now is to make
                room for that in practice.
            </p>
            <p>
                So here is my proposal: begin with the partnership, not a
                product. Build the education, the trust, and the governance
                together, and then see where the relationship and the journey
                take you. What we build is not just technology. Technologies
                will change, data will change; what endures are the
                relationships carried forward by each generation to the future.
                If we build those well, we will have something worth carrying
                between and beyond. Thank you.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
    </Fragment>
</KickoffSession>
